<section id="delivery">
  <div class="wrap dive-phase">
    <div class="dive-phase-label">
      <div class="dive-phase-n">Phase 2</div>
      <div class="dive-phase-when">Month three to the finish line</div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 class="mt-0">Delivery &mdash; the Project Management Office</h2>
      <p>We define strategy as a magnetic field pulling toward a common point &mdash; the force that aligns every decision made across the organization, every day, toward the same outcome. The average professional makes roughly 20 decisions a day; a 30-person team makes around 600. Left to individual judgment, those 600 decisions scatter. A clearly defined, well-communicated strategy is what pulls them into alignment.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A recurring one-hour weekly review of all active actions</li>
        <li>Standard project-management discipline, familiar to virtually every organization</li>
        <li>An external facilitator on the call each week, asking the questions that keep accountability alive</li>
      </ul>
      <p>It&rsquo;s precisely the &ldquo;sticky&rdquo; issues &mdash; the ones that matter but never quite become anyone&rsquo;s full-time priority &mdash; that get resolved once there&rsquo;s a person in the room every week whose job is to ask about them. Several clients have made this a standing part of how they operate, in relationships that have now run for over ten years.</p>
      <p>This is also why we can offer cancel-any-time: once the strategy document is set, typically around the three-month mark, this second half of our value delivery begins. Clients who&rsquo;ve experienced it rarely walk away, because it&rsquo;s precisely where most strategy initiatives elsewhere quietly die.</p>
